n("berry-keating-smooth-count-only","obstruction","Regularised xp models reproduce only the smooth count","Sierra-Rodriguez-Laguna: semiclassical count (E/2 pi)(ln(E/2 pi) - 1) + mu + 2 pi/E; fit constant 0.875007 +- 5.3e-5 (= 7/8). The gamma-phase spectrum theta(E_n) = pi(n - 3/2) misses the zeros by exactly S(T) (rms 0.3108 spacings, correlation 0.9999999994). Fluctuations need primes.","closed","no","no",R("route-31-berry-keating-xp-regularised-2026-10-13"),["trace-formula","explicit-formula"])
n("bbm-growth-class-implication","identity","Bender-Brody-Muller growth classes","psi_s = -zeta(s, x+1), Delta psi_s = x^{-s}, psi_s(0) = -zeta(s): the boundary condition selects all zeros. An off-line zero with sigma > 1/2 gives growth x^{1-sigma}; reality of the point spectrum on a growth-cap domain of order >= x^{1/2} implies RH (one direction; Delta^{-1} ambiguous).","proved","no","yes",R("route-31-berry-keating-xp-regularised-2026-10-13"),["trace-formula"])
n("inverse-spectral-consumes-zeros","obstruction","Inverse spectral potentials consume the ordinates","Wu-Sprung V(x) ~ pi^2 x^2/log^2 x from Abel inversion reproduces smooth points only (rms 0.387 / 0.333). Gauss-Newton potentials match 5000 zeros to 2e-11 discretely, 1-2% of a spacing in the continuum, interpolant-dependent. A 1D Schrodinger Hilbert-Polya operator would force RH and simplicity.","closed","no","no",R("route-32-inverse-spectral-potential-2026-10-13"),["trace-formula"])
n("bogomolny-keating-amplitude-one","measured-phenomenon","Pair-correlation departure from GUE = Bogomolny-Keating at amplitude 1","Data - GUE = a (BK - GUE) with a = 1.0000 +- 0.0008 over 8 windows (T = 5.5e4 to 1e13); diagonal 0.9999 +- 0.0008, oscillating 1.0023 +- 0.0031, Hardy-Littlewood factor 1.0032 +- 0.0067; per-prime amplitudes n = 2..13 detected above 20 sigma. Lambda_B = 1.5731511.","measured","yes","no",R("route-33-arithmetic-rmt-corrections-2026-10-13"),["pair-correlation","euler-product","explicit-formula"],"P31")
n("bk-diagonal-prime-identity","identity","Diagonal term of the pair correlation as a prime sum","G1(r) = (zeta'/zeta)'(1 + ir) - B(ir) = sum_{n = p^k} log^2 p n^{-1-ir}; A'(0) = 0, so the Bogomolny-Keating series in L has no 1/L term. The oscillating part (Hardy-Littlewood factor A) has no proof.","proved","yes","no",R("route-33-arithmetic-rmt-corrections-2026-10-13"),["pair-correlation","euler-product"])
n("de-branges-lagarias-hamiltonian","obstruction","de Branges Hamiltonian of Xi + i Xi' restates RH","Lagarias E = A - iB, A = Xi, B = -Xi' is Hermite-Biehler iff RH. Finite-N chain is the reversed counting-measure Jacobi chain (weights 1/A'(x)^2); H = diag(h, 1-h). E_a(z) = Xi(z + ia) ~ Xi + ia Xi' as a -> 0: H_a positive for every a > 0 is the zero-free-strip ladder T3.","closed","no","yes",R("route-34-de-branges-hamiltonian-xi-2026-10-13"),["de-branges","twin-ladder","zero-free"],"G6")
n("krein-string-total-length","identity","Total length of the Krein string of Xi","Total length 2 sum 1/gamma^2 = xi''(1/2)/xi(1/2) = 0.0462099862308 (measured from 1e6 zeros plus tail: 0.0462099862327). Type tau(x) = int sqrt(det H): tau(1e10) = 0.638-0.654. Infinite-N Hamiltonian exists iff RH (with simple zeros).","measured","no","yes",R("route-34-de-branges-hamiltonian-xi-2026-10-13"),["de-branges"])
n("li-coefficients-certified","obstruction","Li coefficients positive to n = 10^4; no all-n route","lambda_n = n [z^n] log xi(1/(1-z)) > 0 certified for 1 <= n <= 10^4 (Arb); lambda_1 = 0.0230957, osc_1 = gamma_E exactly. Weaker than verified height implies. The 7/8 half-plane gives only |1 - 1/rho|^2 <= 1 + O(1/gamma^2): no all-n positivity.","closed","no","no",R("route-35-certified-li-coefficients-2026-10-13"),["zero-free","explicit-formula"],"P16")
